<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Aljubarrota</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1385">1385</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1383-1385-portuguese-interregnum">1383–1385 Portuguese interregnum</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Aljubarrota was fought on 14 August 1385 in central Portugal, between the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-portugal">John I of Portugal</a>, with English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, and the larger army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-castile">John I of Castile</a>. Using defensive tactics devised by the constable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuno-alvares-pereira">Nuno Álvares Pereira</a>, the Portuguese won decisively. The victory secured Portugal's independence and the Aviz dynasty, and John I built the monastery of Batalha to commemorate it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-aljubarrota.jpg" alt="Battle of Aljubarrota" width="320" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean d'Wavrin (Chronique d'Angleterre) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalha_de_Aljubarrota_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>King Ferdinand I of Portugal died in October 1383 with no son. His daughter Beatrice was to marry Juan I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> under the Treaty of Salvaterra de Magos, which left the Portuguese nobility and the merchants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> discontented. Portugal had no king from 1383 to 1385, in a period known as the 1383–85 Crisis. John, Grand Master of the Military Order of Aviz, was named defender of the realm, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> acclaimed him king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a> on 6 April 1385.</p><p>In April 1384 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuno-alvares-pereira">Nuno Álvares Pereira</a> had defeated a Castilian force at Atoleiros with a defensive infantry square. In May 1385 Juan I invaded with 31,000 men. English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, about 200 longbowmen and some 500 men-at-arms, had arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Álvares Pereira chose a small hill at São Jorge between creeks, and the Portuguese took position there on 14 August 1385. When the Castilians moved to attack the gentler southern slope, the Portuguese turned their line round and dug ditches, pits and caltrops. The army stood with dismounted men-at-arms in the centre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> on the flanks, among them the young nobles of the Lovers' Flank.</p><p>French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> in Castilian service charged first, uphill under arrows and bolts, and was destroyed. Froissart reports that the Portuguese council then decided to kill their prisoners. The main Castilian body was crowded between the creeks and shot at from behind the Portuguese line. About sunset the Castilian standard-bearer fell, and Juan's army fled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>As many as 5,000 more Castilians were killed by local people during the night and next day. Tradition tells of Brites de Almeida, the baker of Aljubarrota, who killed seven soldiers hiding in her bakery, though the story is clouded in legend. John of Portugal offered the survivors amnesty and free passage home. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> decreed mourning until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> 1387. Álvares Pereira beat another Castilian army at Valverde in October 1385, and border skirmishes lasted until the death of Juan I of Castile in 1390.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Aljubarrota” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Castilian galleys, allied with France, defeated an English fleet off La Rochelle in 1372, while an attempt on the Portuguese throne ended at the <b>Battle of Aljubarrota</b> in 1385.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…of 1383–1385 John, Master of Avis, and his constable Nuno Álvares Pereira defeated a much larger Castilian army at the <b>Battle of Aljubarrota</b> on 14 August 1385, with the help of English archers, and the Treaty of Windsor of 1386 began an alliance with England that still exists.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Military</a></li></ul></section>
